<CaseStudy slug="agent-forensics">
  <p class="lead reveal">I kept running Claude Code sessions that went sideways in ways I could not fully explain afterward: a tool-call loop, a retried command that never recovered, a session that just stopped. This is the tool I built to stop guessing, and to do it without keeping the raw transcripts anywhere.</p>

  <h2 class="reveal">What it found</h2>
  <p class="reveal">Run against 189 of my own Claude Code sessions (July to September 2026, 36,480 tool calls), it found at least one pre-registered breakdown pattern in 96 of them: 50.8% (95% Wilson CI 43.7 to 57.8). The most frequent was a timeout, in 62 sessions. Across every session, 896 of 36,480 tool calls (2.5%) returned an error. Half of my own agent sessions hit a known failure mode, which is worth knowing before trusting one unattended.</p>

  <h2 class="reveal">Redacted by construction, not by filtering</h2>
  <p class="reveal">Every transcript goes through one parser that builds a record which can only ever hold counts, durations, booleans, small labels, and HMAC tokens. There is no code path that copies a prompt, a tool input, a tool output, or a file path into the record. Eleven detectors, frozen and hashed before any transcript is read, look for loops, retries, API errors, refusals, dangling calls, hook errors, and timeouts; seven of them form the headline set. A threshold cannot be loosened after seeing a result without the protocol hash changing and the audit catching it.</p>

  <h2 class="reveal">How it is checked</h2>
  <p class="reveal">68 tests pass and the type-check is clean. An audit sweeps everything under the data directory for redaction violations before it can be committed, and a reproduce command re-derives the findings from the committed records offline, failing on any byte-level mismatch. The redaction claim is therefore checkable in the data itself. The source is public: <a href="https://github.com/rgcareer/portfolio-builds/tree/main/packages/agent-forensics">read it and run it yourself</a>.</p>
</CaseStudy>
